@startuml
' Rendered by scripts/build-diagrams.sh. Edit this file, never the SVG beside it.
'
' One neutral grey on a transparent background, so a single SVG stays legible on
' the light theme and the dark one. Nothing here picks a palette, which is what
' lets the site own its own colors.
<style>
document {
  BackgroundColor transparent
}
stateDiagram {
  BackgroundColor transparent
  LineColor #888888
  FontColor #888888
  FontName Helvetica
  FontSize 13

  state {
    BackgroundColor transparent
    LineColor #888888
    FontColor #888888
  }
  note {
    BackgroundColor transparent
    LineColor #888888
    FontColor #888888
  }
  arrow {
    LineColor #888888
    FontColor #888888
  }
  diamond {
    BackgroundColor #888888
    LineColor #888888
  }
}
</style>
skinparam shadowing false

' Two regions rather than one chain, because the two keys are independent: an
' operation is stable AND deprecated in the normal, well-behaved case.
'
' Separated by "||" and not "--". PlantUML's "--" draws a horizontal rule, which
' stacks the regions one above the other; "||" draws a vertical one and puts them
' side by side, which is the shape the prose argues for and the only one that
' shows the two tracks running in parallel rather than in sequence.
state "An operation the document declares" as Operation {

  state "How strong the promise is\n(x-lifecycle)" as Promise {
    [*] --> beta : public, and nothing declared
    [*] --> no_claim : x-audience: internal
    beta --> stable : the author promises it
    no_claim --> stable : an internal endpoint may promise too
    state "no claim" as no_claim
  }

  ||

  state "Whether it is going away\n(deprecated, x-sunset)" as Removal {
    [*] --> current
    current --> deprecated : deprecated: true,\nwhich requires x-sunset
    deprecated --> past_sunset : the date arrives
    state "past its sunset" as past_sunset
  }
}

note bottom of Operation
  The two regions are independent. Stable and deprecated is not a
  contradiction, it is a promise kept to its stated removal date.
end note
@enduml
